  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 1230 AM CST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nPost-frontal rains will continue across the east or southeastern \nhalf of the DVN CWA through mid Sunday morning, before exiting off to \nthe east following the forcing and moisture conveyor flux off to the \neast by mid afternoon. High temps Sunday continue to be a \nchallenging go of it with incoming cold air advection(CAA), clouds \nand initial precip. Many of the 12 hour highs will occur in the \nmorning especially in the east with mild values in the mid to upper \n50s. Then temps to fall later in the day on the nose of blustery \npost-frontal northwesterly sfc winds. Values along and east of the \nMS RVR may range from the mid to upper 50s Sunday morning, but be \nonly in the 40s in the far northwest. If the front slows or speeds \nup at all from the current projections, these temps are subject to \nconsiderable change. Either way, a cloudy, damp, blustery day will \ncontinue to be the story well into Sunday for most areas, and a \nreturn to more \"normal\" November weather will be on the way. Sunday \nnight low temps will be down in the 20s for most areas.\n\nHigh pressure will continue to follow the FROPA and build into the \nregion into Monday, with decreasing north to northwesterly sfc winds \nas the pressure gradient relaxes while the main ridge axis overtakes \nthe MO RVR Valley from the north. \n\nThe freshly arrived cool core Canadian high pressure will remain \nover the region through mid week, resulting in persistently more \nseasonable weather and mainly dry conditions and relatively light \nwinds. Will still have to watch for the potential of northern and \nsouthern stream phasing of wave energy that may try to draw precip \nfrom the north or south Wednesday through Friday, but chances\nare the local forecast area will remain in-between the action\nzones.",
